%N A397315 Table read by antidiagonals: T(n,k) = maximum number of points that can be blacked out of an n X k grid of points so that every rectangle with all four vertices on grid points (tilted rectangles included) is uniquely identifiable from its set of visible vertices; n, k >= 2.
%C A397315 From Problem 001 of Peter Kagey's Open Problem Collection (https://peterkagey.com/problems/001/): black out as many grid points as possible while every rectangle with lattice corners remains identifiable from its visible corners; equivalently, injectivity of the map R -> C(R) \ S over all rectangles.
%C A397315 Equivalent formulation: n*k minus the minimum size of a hitting set of the hypergraph whose edges are the symmetric differences C(R1) triangle C(R2) over distinct rectangle pairs.
%C A397315 T(2,m) = m+1 for m >= 3, with exactly m*2^(m-1) maximum blackouts (see link). T(2,2) = 4 since a single rectangle imposes no constraint. The number of maximum blackouts is given in A399580.
%C A397315 The 5 X 5 maximum blackout is unique up to the symmetries of the square.
%C A397315 All values cross-verified by two independently written implementations, and on small grids by exhaustive search from the definition.
%C A397315 From _Arjun Pemmasani_, Oct 24 2026: (Start)
%C A397315 Theorem: T(3,k) = k+2 for all k >= 3. [Conjectured in the original version of this entry, Sep 02 2026.] A proof is in the Pemmasani link.
%C A397315 The original version of this entry (Sep 02 2026) conjectured that T(n,k) = n+k whenever min(n,k) >= 4, except for T(4,4) = 7 and T(4,5) = 8. This is false. T(4,k) = k+4 for k >= 6, but T(5,8) = 14 and T(6,8) = 15. No grid with fewer points than the 5 X 8 grid has T(n,k) > n+k.
%C A397315 T(5,6), T(5,7), T(6,6), T(6,7), T(6,8) and T(7,7) rest on computer searches with an integer program, SAT solvers and a column search (see the Pemmasani link). The SAT results have LRAT certificates, each accepted by two checkers.
%C A397315 For each fixed n, the valid blackouts of the n X k grids, written column by column as words, form a regular language (see the Pemmasani link).
%C A397315 T(7,8) = 16: a SAT search finds no valid blackout of 17 points, in two encodings on different solvers, with a checked LRAT certificate, and one of 16 points exists. (End)
%H A397315 Peter Kagey, <a href="https://peterkagey.com/problems/001/">Open Problem Collection, Problem 001</a>.
%H A397315 Arjun Pemmasani, <a href="https://github.com/apemm/Kagey-Problems/blob/main/kagey-problems/problem001/jis/pemmasani.pdf">Maximum Blackouts That Keep Every Lattice Rectangle Identifiable</a>, preprint, 2026.
%H A397315 Arjun Pemmasani, <a href="https://github.com/apemm/Kagey-Problems/tree/main/kagey-problems/problem001">Solvers, exhaustive verification, and pre-registration ledger for Problem 001</a> (GitHub repository).
%F A397315 From _Arjun Pemmasani_, Oct 24 2026: (Start)
%F A397315 T(n,k) = T(k,n).
%F A397315 T(n,k) >= n+k-1.
%F A397315 T(n,k) <= k + A002620(n) for k >= 3.
%F A397315 T(2,k) = k+1 and T(3,k) = k+2 for k >= 3.
%F A397315 T(4,k) = k+4 for k >= 6.
%F A397315 T(5,k) = k+6 for k >= 8.
%F A397315 T(6,k) = k+9 for k >= 15, and k+7 <= T(6,k) <= k+8 for 9 <= k <= 14.
%F A397315 For each fixed n >= 2, T(n,k) - k is eventually periodic in k. (End)
